    decision:
      "Continuation of the same scheduled routine on claude/upbeat-dijkstra-tg1n4z, picked up at origin/main tip 661ea488 (PR #921, empty-backlog-second-consecutive-clean-sweep, merged this session). Confirmed baseline green: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean). A git fetch --prune sweep and mcp__github__list_pull_requests found no other live sibling PR beyond the unrelated stale #202.",
    rationale:
      "With two consecutive clean sweeps behind it, a research agent was dispatched with three explicitly different tactics rather than another pass over the same five tiers: a stuck-sibling-branch check (any local/remote branch with unmerged commits sitting idle), a deep single-component keyboard/screen-reader accessibility audit of src/app/dashboard/leads/leads-client.tsx (the largest client component in the repo, never audited end-to-end before), and a re-check of the five gated NEXT_STEPS scaffold items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) for any external blocker having resolved. The branch check and the gated-scaffold check both came back clean: no stuck branches, and all five items remain genuinely operator/legal/infra-blocked with nothing actionable. The accessibility audit found the file has zero role=\"dialog\"/aria-modal/<dialog> elements anywhere (no true modals, so focus-trap bugs are structurally impossible), all ~68 onClick handlers are on real <button> elements, all 8 disclosure toggles correctly pair aria-expanded/aria-controls, and the focus-restore-on-unmount logic already has multiple documented fixed races visible in its own comments. The one substantive finding: of roughly 64 action buttons gated on the single shared `isPending` from this component's one `useTransition()`, only 3 (\"Import leads\"/\"Save Lead\"/\"Save notes\") change their visible label during the pending state; the other ~61 (\"Billing\", \"Upsell\", \"Send Email 1\", etc.) simply disable with no textual or live-region change, characterized by the agent as \"a genuine WCAG 4.1.3-adjacent nice-to-have, not a keyboard dead-end or trap.\"\n\nIndependently re-verified the finding itself (grep -n 'isPending ?' leads-client.tsx confirmed exactly the same 3 of ~64 matches) and then traced two levels deeper before deciding whether to ship a fix, because the obvious shallow fix - copying the existing `{isPending ? \"X...\" : \"X\"}` ternary onto a fourth button - turned out not to actually close the gap. First: `isPending` is one process-wide `useTransition()` (line 480) shared across the entire component, already the subject of a large existing comment block a few lines below it (dashboard-focus-restore-async-transition-race) documenting that a button loses focus at the exact moment it becomes disabled during an async transition, because React holds `isPending` true for the whole awaited operation rather than just its synchronous portion. That means whatever label sits on a button at the instant it disables is not reliably announced via a focus-based mechanism in the first place - the screen reader's focus is already moving away as the state flips, independent of what the label says. Second, and decisive: the 3 buttons that do change label have no `aria-label` override, so their visible text IS their accessible name and the ternary genuinely changes what would be announced if focus were retained; but the ~61 row-scoped action buttons (Billing, Upsell, etc.) all pass a *static* accessible name through `dashboardRowActionAriaLabel(action, businessName, slug)` (src/lib/leads/dashboard-row-aria-labels.ts) that does not vary with `isPending` - aria-label always wins over visible text for the accessible name, so adding a `{isPending ? \"Opening…\" : \"Billing\"}` ternary to one of these buttons would change only the sighted-user-visible label while the announced name stays frozen at \"Billing — Acme Co (acme)\" throughout. Shipping that would be a cosmetic-only change masquerading as an accessibility fix, not a real one.\n\nThe actually-correct fix is a single shared aria-live \"operation in progress\" announcement decoupled from any individual button's disabled/focus state (so it survives the focus-loss race above), not per-button label or aria-label edits - a properly systemic, one-shared-mechanism change that deserves its own carefully scoped iteration rather than being bolted on as a copy-paste of the existing three-button pattern. Separately confirmed via `grep -i testing-library package.json` (no match) and a repo-wide grep for `@testing-library/react` (no match anywhere in src/) that this codebase has zero DOM-rendering component-test infrastructure - every existing test (all 215 files) covers pure/lib logic only, none render JSX. Every fix shipped this session has been proven via the git-stash regression-catching technique against a real test assertion; a client-rendered accessibility fix in a 5,992-line, entirely untested-at-the-DOM-level component cannot be proven that way without first standing up a new render-test toolchain from scratch, which is itself a structural, repo-wide decision (new dependency, new test convention) out of scope for \"one focused iteration\" and not something to bundle silently into what should be a small fix.\n\nDeferred rather than shipped, with two named prerequisites for a future session that wants to close this: (1) decide whether to invest in @testing-library/react + jsdom render-test infra, or explicitly accept a git-stash-exemption carve-out for pure-JSX-label/aria changes (mirroring the fact that the existing 3-button precedent itself has never had a regression test); (2) design the fix as a shared, focus-independent aria-live status region rather than per-button label/aria-label edits, given the focus-loss-on-disable race already documented in this same file. This is the third consecutive iteration with no shipped code, but distinct from the two prior clean sweeps: this one surfaced a real, traceable finding and reasoned explicitly why implementing it now would either ship a cosmetic no-op or violate this session's own testing discipline, rather than finding nothing at all.\n\nsrc/lib/process/business-decisions.ts - this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx - the accompanying ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change - all five gated items remain operator/legal/infra-blocked, unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to 661ea488 (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#921).\n\nFile-size check per AGENT_PROTOCOL's re-archive note: prompt_process.tsx (~248KB) and business-decisions.ts (~201KB) both measured via wc -c before this entry - both comfortably under the 262,144-byte trigger, no re-archive needed this round.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run full suite (unchanged - no product/test code touched) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings). No dev-server smoke check needed - this iteration touched only the process log.",
